## 4. Improvements (new capability deltas)

Stated as **requirements**, not models.

### 4.1 Adverse-selection-aware pricing
Quotes must reflect that **winning a competitive RFQ is informative** — the engine prices defensively against being picked off, rather than treating edge as a fixed add-on to a reference mid.

### 4.2 Confidence-driven spread sizing
The engine must attach a **confidence** to every quote and **widen when confidence is low, tighten when high**. The current "reference price missing → refuse to quote" behavior becomes a **graded** gate: price with an explicit uncertainty that both *gates* and *sizes* the spread.

### 4.3 Continuous inventory skew (replace the threshold veto)
Position must drive a **continuous skew** — more aggressive on the offsetting side, progressively backing off as the limit approaches — instead of a hard "≥ X mm → stop quoting" cliff. The position limit becomes a **soft penalty**, not a wall. `MFS-Desire` becomes a *driver* of skew, not just a displayed column.

### 4.4 Aggregate in-flight risk budget
A live **exposure budget across the entire set of currently-open quotes** (sector, rating bucket, spread DV01, HY beta), so correlated names resolving in the same binned/auction window cannot collectively breach portfolio risk. Quotes are **auto-widened or pulled** as the budget fills.

### 4.5 System-level circuit breakers
Beyond per-quote clamps: **desk-level breakers** that trip on anomalous behavior (e.g. abnormal off-market-clamp rate, abnormal quote/cancel rate). A protection that fires constantly is itself an upstream-fault signal.

### 4.6 Hedge-aware quoting
Willingness to quote, and how tightly, must depend on **current hedge capacity and marginal hedge cost** (CDX / ETF / duration). The existing ETF rails feed the RFQ skew so the quoter tightens when risk can be laid off cheaply.

### 4.7 Drift-aware skew (consolidated pressure signal)
The engine consumes a **single consolidated market-pressure signal** (fusing TRACE pressure, dealer-run/axe imbalance, issuer equity, ETF flow) and skews toward the **predicted short-horizon move** over the quote's live window.

### 4.8 List / portfolio pricing with netting
Pricing a **list coherently** so buys offset sells and the aggregate required edge is **below sum-of-parts**, rather than pricing each RFQ in isolation.

### 4.9 Counterparty-aware skew
Track **per-counterparty behavior** (do their fills predict adverse moves?) and skew per name. The current star-rating is the seed of this.

### 4.10 Make/take co-optimization
The responder's quote can account for whether the engine can likely **take** the same name cheaply — make/take is an **input to the quote**, not just a side feature.

