## 5. Data & Storage Requirements

The OMS must persist, immutably and timestamped:

- Every **RFQ** (full inbound payload) and every **quote version** sent.
- Every **guard evaluation** and **do-not-quote decision** (with the reason).
- Every **parameter / bias / protection change**, attributed.
- **Cover and outcome data** for every inquiry (won / lost / done-away / timed-out, and where it traded).
- **RFQ-ID → full input lineage** retrievable on demand.

Where venues don't report price/spread, the system should **match RFQ data to TRACE** to reconstruct levels.

---

## 6. Architecture Requirement — Event-Sourced Spine

- The OMS is the **event store and single system of record**; the responder and dashboard are **readers** of it.
- Every RFQ, quote, guard evaluation, and parameter change is an **immutable event**.
- This enables **replay**: historical flow can be re-run against any candidate pricing/guard/parameter configuration, so **every dial is backtestable**.
- This also **collapses today's fragmentation** (separate responder UI, credit2025, Credit-OMS, dash app) onto one spine.
- *Note:* this is effectively impossible to retrofit onto a snapshot-state OMS later — it is a day-one decision.

---

## 7. Surfaces / UI Requirements

- **Always-on trader dashboard**: live blotter + status, per-inquiry inputs (edge · fill-likelihood · ref-mid · confidence), manual override / pull, do-not-quote toggles, bias (wider ↔ aggressive), protections (ECN / dealer / off-market TRACE), RFQ-ID → lineage.
- **Per-CUSIP drill-down**: TRACE prints, ECN stack, dealer quotes, BBG runs, reference-price history.
- **Diagnostics**: trade hit-rates and volume/count over time by sector / rating; clear "why not AQ-ed" explanations; ability to grab an RFQ-ID and inspect its full inputs.
- Everything routed through **one dash-class interface** (the legacy single-inquiry responder UI is too inflexible to be the primary surface).

---

## 8. Out of Scope (owned in the quant / modelling spec)

Explicitly **not** specified here, by design:

- How fill-likelihood, conditional fair value, or confidence are **estimated**.
- How the consolidated market-pressure signal is **constructed**.
- The functional form of inventory skew, hedge cost, or counterparty toxicity.
- Any pricing math, calibration method, or statistical estimator.

This spec defines the **contract** those models must satisfy (inputs available, outputs required, where they plug in) — not the models themselves.
